! Storage protection: the ADDRESS BOUNDARY REGISTERS and the boundary
! control bit.  The companion to storage_protection.strap, which covers
! the permanently protected area (words 1-3) only.
!
! THE RULES, all from Reference Manual p.31-32.
!
! p.31, "Definition of Monitored Area": "A core storage area is defined
! by placing an address in each of two address boundary registers.
! This area includes the word specified by the address in the lower
! boundary register and all words in subsequent locations up to, but
! not including, the address in the upper boundary register.  The
! address in the upper boundary register is normally larger than that
! in the lower boundary register.  If it is equal or smaller, no words
! are contained in the core storage area.  The upper boundary and lower
! boundary registers are located in bits 0-17 and 32-49, respectively,
! of storage location 3."  UPPER IS NAMED FIRST AND SITS LOWER IN THE
! WORD; that order is the trap this program pins.
!
! p.31: "The boundary control bit, bit 57 of storage location 3,
! determines which condition will cause an address monitoring signal.
! When the boundary control bit is zero, a signal is obtained for a
! comparand inside the specified area.  When the boundary control bit
! is one, a signal is obtained for a comparand outside the specified
! area."  BOTH POLARITIES ARE EXERCISED BELOW; one of them alone leaves
! the sense of the compare free.
!
! p.31: "The monitoring of references to locations 0-31 is independent
! of the contents of the boundary registers... References to locations
! 0 and 4 through 31, inclusive, never cause an address monitoring
! signal."  INDEPENDENT, not additional -- with the control bit set,
! words 4-31 are outside every area a program can name, so a rule
! written as "permanently protected OR outside the area" would signal
! on the special registers.  Phase B checks word 4 for exactly that.
!
! p.32: "An address which actuates indicator AD, address invalid, is
! not compared with the contents of the boundary registers and cannot
! actuate any of the address alarm indicators."  The last block stores
! past the installed core with the control bit set, where a missing
! exemption would raise DS.
!
! HOW THE BOUNDARIES ARE LOADED, and why this idiom.  SSIP's SIP16
! resets word 2 with "LVI,$X13,INT" then "SV,$X13,2.0" -- a bit address
! into an index value field, then a half-word store.  The same pair
! works for the boundaries because the fields line up: SV writes the
! 24-bit value into bits 0-23 of its half-word, so a WORD-aligned bit
! address lands with its word number in bits 0-17 of that half -- which
! is where both boundary registers live.  And SV's low seven bits are
! read back from storage, so a store to the LOWER half (bits 32-63)
! leaves bit 57, the control bit, alone.  The register layout is built
! for this sequence.
!
! AND THE STORES HAPPEN WITH THE SYSTEM DISABLED, because word 3 is
! itself permanently protected -- SIP16's "BD" is commented "DISABLE
! BEFORE RESET OF PROTECTED AREA".
!
! PHASE A USES BIT 0 OF EACH TEST WORD AND PHASE B USES BIT 1, so no
! check in the second phase can pass on a bit the first phase left set.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD: hand-written from p.31 and p.32.  No listing in this
! collection sets a boundary register to a non-zero value.

        SLC, (8)4100.0
!
! ---- phase A: control bit 0, a signal INSIDE the area --------------
START   BD, $+.32                    ! disable: word 3 is protected
        LVI, $X13, TBL               ! the interruption table origin
        SV,  $X13, $IA               ! word 2 is protected too, so disabled
        LVI, $X13, WLOW              ! the lower boundary's bit address
        SV,  $X13, $LB               ! lower boundary := WLOW  (bits 32-49)
        LVI, $X13, WABOVE            ! the upper boundary's bit address
        SV,  $X13, $UB               ! upper boundary := WABOVE (bits 0-17)
        BBZ, 3.57, $+1.0             ! control bit := 0: signal INSIDE
        BE,  $+.32                   ! enable the interrupt system
!
! below the area: the store lands
        BB1, WBELOW, $+1.0
        BZB, WBELOW, $               ! hang if off -- the store must have landed
        BB,  6.0, $                  ! hang if DS's routine ran
! at the lower boundary: INSIDE, so suppressed.  Pins >= against >.
        BB1, WLOW, $+1.0
        BB,  WLOW, $                 ! hang if on -- the store must be suppressed
        BZB, 6.0, $                  ! hang if DS's routine did not run
        BBZ, 6.0, $+1.0              !   ... and clear it for the next check
! one below the upper boundary: still inside
        BB1, WHIGH, $+1.0
        BB,  WHIGH, $
        BZB, 6.0, $                  ! hang if DS's routine did not run
        BBZ, 6.0, $+1.0              !   ... and clear it for the next check
! at the upper boundary: OUTSIDE, "up to, but not including".  Pins <
!   against <=, which is the whole content of the half-open interval.
        BB1, WABOVE, $+1.0
        BZB, WABOVE, $
        BB,  6.0, $                  ! the store must NOT have been suppressed
!
! ---- phase B: control bit 1, a signal OUTSIDE the area -------------
        BD, $+.32                    ! disable again to reach word 3
        BB1, 3.57, $+1.0             ! control bit := 1: signal OUTSIDE
        BE,  $+.32
!
! inside the area: now the store lands
        BB1, WLOW+.1, $+1.0
        BZB, WLOW+.1, $
        BB,  6.0, $                  ! the store must NOT have been suppressed
! below the area: outside, so suppressed
        BB1, WBELOW+.1, $+1.0
        BB,  WBELOW+.1, $
        BZB, 6.0, $                  ! hang if DS's routine did not run
        BBZ, 6.0, $+1.0              !   ... and clear it for the next check
! at the upper boundary: outside, so suppressed -- the same address
!   that was PERMITTED in phase A, which is how the polarity is pinned
!   rather than the compare
        BB1, WABOVE+.1, $+1.0
        BB,  WABOVE+.1, $
        BZB, 6.0, $                  ! hang if DS's routine did not run
        BBZ, 6.0, $+1.0              !   ... and clear it for the next check
!
! word 4 is a special register: p.31 says 4 through 31 NEVER signal,
! and with the control bit set it lies outside the area.  The store
! must land and DS must stay off.
        BB1, 4.0, $+1.0
        BZB, 4.0, $
        BB,  6.0, $                  ! the store must NOT have been suppressed
!
! word 3 is permanently protected "regardless of... the setting of the
! boundary control bit".  Its bit 0 is the upper boundary's high bit,
! so a store that is not suppressed would also move the boundary.
        BB1, 3.0, $+1.0
        BZB, 6.0, $                  ! hang if DS's routine did not run
        BBZ, 6.0, $+1.0              !   ... and clear it for the next check
!
! past the installed core: address-invalid, exempt from address
! monitoring (p.32).  Outside the area, so a missing exemption raises
! DS here.
        BB1, (8)300000.0, $+1.0
        BB,  6.0, $                  ! the store must NOT have been suppressed
        B, PASS
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the monitored area and its neighbours ------------------------
! An explicit SLC: DD emits at the location counter, and code that
! grows would otherwise overrun its own data.
        SLC, (8)4300.0
WBELOW  DD, 0                        ! below the lower boundary
WLOW    DD, 0                        ! == the lower boundary, first word in
WHIGH   DD, 0                        ! == upper-1, last word in
WABOVE  DD, 0                        ! == the upper boundary, first word out
!
! ---- the interruption table ----------------------------------------
! A protected STORE raises DS, and DS is permanently masked (p.48), so
! an ENABLED machine takes an interruption rather than leaving the
! indicator for the program to read.  The violation is therefore
! observed through this routine, which is how the machine reports it.
!
! ITS MARKER IS WORD 6, and that is forced.  p.31: locations "0 and 4
! through 31, inclusive, never cause an address monitoring signal".
! With the control bit set, EVERY address outside the monitored area
! signals -- so a marker in ordinary core would be suppressed and raise
! DS again, and the machine would interrupt forever.  Word 6 is in the
! carve-out and cannot.  (Word 4 is already this program's witness THAT
! the carve-out works, so the marker uses a different one.)
        SLC, (8)4500.0
TBL     DD, 0                        ! entry N is at TBL+N words
        SLC, (8)4523.0
DSRTN   BB1, 6.0, $+1.0              ! entry 19 = DS
        END, START
